{
  "slug": "intersection-of-two-arrays",
  "title": "Intersection of Two Arrays",
  "topic": "arrays-hashing",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "pattern": "Hash set membership with deduplicated, sorted output",
  "statement": "Given two integer arrays `nums1` and `nums2`, return every value that occurs in both arrays.\n\nA value that appears several times in either array appears only once in the answer, and the answer must be sorted in ascending order. If the arrays share no value, return an empty array.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums1.length <= 200`, `1 <= nums2.length <= 200`, `0 <= nums1[i] <= 1000` and `0 <= nums2[i] <= 1000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums1 = [0], nums2 = [0]",
      "output": "[0]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums1 = [667, 553], nums2 = [992]",
      "output": "[]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums1: number[], nums2: number[])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "A value belongs in the answer when it exists in the first array and also in the second one.",
    "Scanning the other array for every candidate costs O(n*m). What structure answers \"have I seen this value?\" in constant time instead?",
    "Load the first array into a set, walk the second array and keep only the values present in that set, deduplicate with a second set, then sort numerically."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force takes each value of nums1 that it has not already accepted and scans the whole of nums2 looking for it, which costs O(n*m) comparisons, then sorts the survivors and scans the partial result to deduplicate. The optimal loads nums1 into a hash set so each membership test is O(1) and filters nums2 through it, dropping matches into a second set that deduplicates for free. Only the final numeric sort is left to pay for.",
    "why_optimal": "Every element of both arrays has to be examined at least once, so \u03a9(n + m) work is unavoidable. A hash set makes each lookup O(1), so the filter pass is O(n + m) and the only extra cost is sorting the k distinct common values, which the statement requires; with values \u2264 1000 and lengths \u2264 200 the counting-sort alternative would also be O(n + m + 1000), but O(k log k) with k \u2264 min(n, m) is no worse in practice at these bounds. The quadratic brute force is genuinely slower because it re-scans nums2 for every candidate.",
    "pitfalls": "Calling JavaScript's default `sort()` with no comparator, which sorts lexicographically and leaves [10, 2] as [10, 2] instead of [2, 10]; forgetting deduplication so a value repeated in both arrays is emitted twice; writing `if (nums2[j])` or `if w:` instead of an explicit equality test, which treats 0 as a miss even though 0 is inside the allowed value range; collecting the intersection in an array with `push` and no membership check, or sorting before deduplicating so equal values are adjacent and easy to mistake for duplicates."
  },
